h the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/89/898075d0f74bdfcbab28e50ee327770400f6f58d82728cd8bf55cc75e852d161.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} {:answer/id #uuid "3e12d6c1-cf51-5a79-9824-a889289525f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71ea03ce-0bea-5a33-b768-531593cecfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437710} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9705fa0-051d-5108-9f33-8e0b95bf6771" :answer-field/key "$F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71524ab4-c1fe-56a5-b9b0-5f1247ce034e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437712} {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9809981f-9ddb-5aa8-be88-9c61eb732ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437713} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bc640c-07ec-54b1-ad03-d8c8b9f0a1f6" :answer-field/key "$G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425e03bd-f65f-5c5d-a11e-3e1e0b0bfa52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437714} {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6312d848-ea3c-5408-9f3b-a4a431e27732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437715} :answer-field/id #uuid "482a8231-c447-5ba1-b319-c14d7a80bd76" :answer-field/key "$H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc1786af-49ed-5f49-98af-90852a6f6e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123984" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$; $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57190c25-01e9-52f1-a602-8b6b3c0b0030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257533} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfeddeb-7431-5e49-a9dd-7f38f3f510f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257898} {:answer/id #uuid "83f96867-7578-5dde-bcc0-b2a067859fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257951} {:answer/id #uuid "e808abd0-4e4a-50c9-b1de-e375e94dc248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258794} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0ebf91-bb0c-5cda-9ad5-c786f1c45f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0ebf91-bb0c-5cda-9ad5-c786f1c45f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258855} :answer-field/id #uuid "df48032b-0934-5e84-9809-b7bc5cdb5be7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7768381-49c0-5755-a526-0e09cbb087b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123993" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{3}-ay)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-2x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{3}-ay,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-a & =3{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ -a & =-2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14db70f5-df8f-5f08-a150-3a466567a842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256971} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb1fb64-ec36-5925-bee7-1a5e8272a7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257201} {:answer/id #uuid "61f28b33-098c-5f5d-9ab0-c30766af1628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257644} {:answer/id #uuid "7533dde9-b1e2-58c9-81d7-b484f86dcbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257810} {:answer/id #uuid "9813db15-8d6c-5d3c-b904-d7c6bd10d9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9813db15-8d6c-5d3c-b904-d7c6bd10d9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258123} :answer-field/id #uuid "272f8a51-e7d1-5e59-aadf-b74351e207b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca502a8a-0b55-5b4d-8a4b-58f7420e37da" :question/math-academy-id "q-123998" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{2}+2xy)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2axy)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{2}+2xy,\\,Q={x}^{2}+2axy.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y+2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+2ay.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+2x & =2x+2ay \\\\ 2y & =2ay \\\\ 2y-2ay & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-a) & =0 \\\\ 1-a & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f02a01-13bc-56b7-b177-ccb430449d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871} {:answer/id #uuid "27841610-a8b4-5bab-82b2-27e5466d5850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257130} {:answer/id #uuid "470598a5-e320-5126-88c8-0994c3641e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257403} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aadeb0-045b-5aca-85eb-0efc92490277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258337} {:answer/id #uuid "c1adda1d-9da5-5d62-bd41-e44c2f642fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09f02a01-13bc-56b7-b177-ccb430449d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871} :answer-field/id #uuid "def33358-ee55-5273-82f4-3c6232c5e848" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "652885e3-f092-5423-886c-a33306bd20ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-124000" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(ax{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=ax{y}^{2}+1,\\,Q=4{x}^{2}y+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2axy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8xy.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2axy & =8xy \\\\ 2axy-8xy & =0 \\\\ 2(a-4)xy & =0 \\\\ a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equality must be satisfied everywhere on $D,$ we conclude that $a-4=0,$ that is, $a=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f626fd-2f98-540d-b753-edd9dc174baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448212} {:answer/id #uuid "78e59729-2e4b-504b-ba62-41d462cf8ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e59729-2e4b-504b-ba62-41d462cf8ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448213} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb686abc-beb6-5d11-86ca-7abdbef18ce5" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71683e7c-6322-5a71-ac90-c19b267a6348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448214} {:answer/id #uuid "f00e6185-a73b-52ef-a41b-d1ed69644032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71683e7c-6322-5a71-ac90-c19b267a6348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448214} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f558bf-ebd2-557b-87cd-b12c76af718a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4525889a-55b7-5233-90f9-f32156fe811b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448216} {:answer/id #uuid "bd070a21-a13b-5f48-b881-ba9cd24e8e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd070a21-a13b-5f48-b881-ba9cd24e8e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448217} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eee062f-dc2e-585b-aa83-7624e11d3b88" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "769cb3f6-44e5-5633-bebf-2f89e5fbe161" :question/math-academy-id "q-125723" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35348fe3-9c3a-5329-8e93-1436583e7d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257251} {:answer/id #uuid "75b868e6-319b-563d-831f-92bee29e5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257815} {:answer/id #uuid "8988ff7c-7ded-5728-ab29-45c3901aa9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257990} {:answer/id #uuid "b84a0627-05af-5fa3-8be1-e5e0905f69e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258394} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe19d67-ace6-5f6f-971e-e78991d0fb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe19d67-ace6-5f6f-971e-e78991d0fb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258825} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac9611f-eb21-5ce8-9755-c7ccbc9751f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5064d3f6-e78c-56b7-8d2f-396471e7bc8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125726" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-3,1)$ to $(1,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-3,1)$ and $B(1,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-2)-f(-3,1) \\\\  & =(1)^{2}(-2)-(-3)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =-2-9 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046fdfb7-5908-5b1c-90e7-05dd1219d510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256819} {:answer/id #uuid "0494c2e3-4f93-51c9-97b4-abdfd2bbd422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256820} {:answer/id #uuid "0c87d320-18b0-5f77-bb7b-34ad58ca38b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc2089f-6561-53d8-b5ee-acdc74d9f43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257182} {:answer/id #uuid "c02c18c1-a46b-54b2-8cf2-af2aecbb3d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c87d320-18b0-5f77-bb7b-34ad58ca38b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f5a55be-ee74-53ab-9b52-5cbae09ff6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d312ac8-d0a5-5f87-aa22-804248c3f5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125732" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-\\pi ,0)$ to $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-\\pi ,0)$ and $B(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(-\\pi ,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(1-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15deefdc-05ae-5086-a5f0-c1dc6044e9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256977} {:answer/id #uuid "2247900f-b21b-584e-a22b-427516ec3d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257062} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d5949-bfc8-5763-bd36-6a74f995a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257566} {:answer/id #uuid "7187435a-9b2a-5138-bdc3-8e258afbb442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8bcb5c-084b-52f8-b5f5-1853ba91623b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7187435a-9b2a-5138-bdc3-8e258afbb442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} :answer-field/id #uuid "568ddf7f-5eb1-53a7-8971-663ff621802f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30e624bb-f7e4-561a-be9c-3d2d751b4f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-125735" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,1)$ to $(0,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,1)$ and $B(0,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,3)-f(-2,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{0}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1}{(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b012eb-6902-5171-a714-6e9adcc3fdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257145} {:answer/id #uuid "729cf2e2-8525-5159-a943-e12c5d56a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257791} {:answer/id #uuid "744ef8ea-157c-58d8-8279-46f596d5d3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257800} {:answer/id #uuid "a79e3f8e-8a0c-50b2-a4c1-c3b8e88aed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dba790-e01b-5073-82cd-206cb42c4124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a79e3f8e-8a0c-50b2-a4c1-c3b8e88aed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} :answer-field/id #uuid "82163bfb-0b92-5277-b853-9c511755cd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "536580f0-4841-5ab4-989a-c614cdea00c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125737" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+z.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,3)$ to $(0,-1,2)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,3)$ and $B(0,-1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-1,2)-f(1,0,3) \\\\  & =({0}^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2)-({1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc29811d-c37a-50df-aab4-72f93f8520df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448218} {:answer/id #uuid "8762f157-eaa2-597a-9b1d-243605b3808f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8762f157-eaa2-597a-9b1d-243605b3808f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448219} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b6e35dc-687e-57d2-8e8c-8130f1de0609" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216c1876-088b-541b-bc8b-3b55c9ff28ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448220} {:answer/id #uuid "8011e624-2484-5b38-9b4d-6861b9854e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8011e624-2484-5b38-9b4d-6861b9854e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448221} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3df687e-6b72-5f94-9d87-c3dd151a791a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69af186-96bc-5342-b8ef-4f56e480d997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448222} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd53bb0-0f24-5305-b990-ee10ae1a3d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd53bb0-0f24-5305-b990-ee10ae1a3d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448223} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e4d753-8f46-5591-adeb-e05bc4133b31" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "230c0014-f97a-5ec3-b394-cdb780e495b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125738" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨2x{y}^{2},\\,2y{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,8)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}-{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 64-1 \\\\  & =255.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=255,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04edc601-2930-52d5-862b-aa70c1a53004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256824} {:answer/id #uuid "123bf47b-36cc-59d7-9d17-a22088107f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256948} {:answer/id #uuid "7d949795-b889-5e52-9f41-bc81ed8d37b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257892} {:answer/id #uuid "88dd0768-8c63-597d-8960-8bce308cab84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257983} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a0d9dc-f41e-5996-a270-62b9910bf70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a0d9dc-f41e-5996-a270-62b9910bf70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4bee5c-5730-5fd9-a144-30c0b35fffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8bad440-9dfc-5258-b308-3c0a302cd41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125739" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,2,0)$ to $(-2,3,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,2,0)$ and $B(-2,3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-2,3,1)-f(-1,2,0) \\\\  & =(3(1)-(-2))-(2(0)-(-1)) \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0625084b-e9ca-5f9d-9edb-7cd203c49e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256835} {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} {:answer/id #uuid "767cc64e-5a0a-54f8-b56e-81ea5d23171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257822} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf9ffb-d8c0-51f0-a661-b433dc2b60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257878} {:answer/id #uuid "e0403918-536a-524a-8c14-ab0744be6702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b90cfc-e328-5b69-bfa9-ca822accdfd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157b91e2-9d0a-5ac1-ac9e-d1e250dcce18" :question/math-academy-id "q-125748" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(1,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,-1)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{{1}^{2}}-(1){e}^{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285c7e2e-81c7-5140-afa6-5d48dd94f80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257139} {:answer/id #uuid "7039e856-80c4-5824-9fee-0f0b696a8386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257767} {:answer/id #uuid "90a8ee2a-31c8-5953-8c8f-bd772ae76f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258056} {:answer/id #uuid "a257066d-bc8d-5c17-85a4-eee5753abd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258203} {:answer/id #uuid "b21226a1-2e58-530b-acce-57a13a3da92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a8ee2a-31c8-5953-8c8f-bd772ae76f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258056} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bacd285-b375-50bb-ac23-bccaee4c52d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b53272f7-7642-5d0e-bc45-fe6cf9e78380" :question/math-academy-id "q-125749" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,0)$ to $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06828943-2517-5788-afbf-83f2f355cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256836} {:answer/id #uuid "a816432d-4895-55c2-953a-1699f10ab4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258249} {:answer/id #uuid "c83059f8-61f6-50d7-9ebe-a77e51cd568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258528} {:answer/id #uuid "f86cf986-18fe-5093-aa80-ac15fc51982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7fca20-5bdf-5366-9789-fd9cfe5d4ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f86cf986-18fe-5093-aa80-ac15fc51982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e700bf1-abf6-54f9-a024-9c52cdfe7394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0148bc8-aabc-5dbc-868a-3905d42c3ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-125751" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}-2x)\\,i+2xy\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(1,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b58465-8414-5bed-8d37-393ef01e3891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256823} {:answer/id #uuid "66e6bb30-d10f-573f-8bfa-381d4851b62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a27f3bf-e79b-581d-88ef-8749ca86f78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258139} {:answer/id #uuid "b910eb27-8f1a-5e45-a7ff-d26555379851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258402} {:answer/id #uuid "f726b334-cfb4-5c99-b22c-166e98d7ab2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f726b334-cfb4-5c99-b22c-166e98d7ab2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258947} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef527c4-bfa6-508b-adc5-32b1bdd9e534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5993ea8-c19e-5b1a-a8cf-e4077a8ec7f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-125753" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,0)$ to $(2,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,0)$ and $B(2,1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={x}^{2}{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(-1,0) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}){e}^{1}-(-1)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\  & =4e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10794cd8-12ac-5cb8-b8c1-b910715a1350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a489fae-a9d9-5967-9682-2c7dc62d72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257155} {:answer/id #uuid "c6210f7f-4a94-5eae-829e-7c2ebbb2ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258514} {:answer/id #uuid "f07868a6-417c-5ea4-8b12-1af3e2b84607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258874} {:answer/id #uuid "f623dcd4-0339-5e15-b9d6-9476f7a218e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f623dcd4-0339-5e15-b9d6-9476f7a218e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932} :answer-field/id #uuid "28961929-f635-5865-9f98-b89b6ca896ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088dda9c-46fc-5710-8c3d-c31a5fce7d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-125755" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=12{x}^{3}\\,i-2\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,3)$ to $(0,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,3)$ and $B(0,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(0,-1)-f(-1,3) \\\\  & =(3(0)^{4}-2(-1))-(3(-1)^{4}-2(3)) \\\\  & =2-(3-6) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1083ce4a-9e9c-58dc-9eef-3be7032b992a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256934} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae56aac-5cd5-538c-adf9-3408e8befc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257161} {:answer/id #uuid "4b406a61-b318-531d-a478-8db71899ab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257438} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0ce09-70e2-55ee-a190-fa6ac4c5fe13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257757} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe44531-11b0-5179-ba8b-37ee8a667e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb0ce09-70e2-55ee-a190-fa6ac4c5fe13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257757} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68edff1-3c68-5b0d-98d0-86374dc452df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8758b7de-dff9-5980-9e4d-360f6523aac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125757" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3y-x)\\,i+3x\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3xy-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(3(2)(-2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(3(0)(1)-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-12-2-0 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c2c453b-fdb8-55cf-afb7-1e908849cbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448224} {:answer/id #uuid "ae479600-ccc1-5572-b350-19d3354ed4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae479600-ccc1-5572-b350-19d3354ed4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448225} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da80a74-a157-519f-8264-e73a6be4a502" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b93c59-2861-5c27-97be-936d2d58613c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448226} {:answer/id #uuid "72e1316f-268d-5f09-9717-2db567645bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e1316f-268d-5f09-9717-2db567645bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e8f95a7-4ae1-5ce1-a2b8-93596df2c4fb" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{1}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ee1943-12e7-5a8e-81ba-c5c9f3220681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448228} {:answer/id #uuid "e385618f-2b72-5771-a5f9-a5240fbcf759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ee1943-12e7-5a8e-81ba-c5c9f3220681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448228} :answer-field/id #uuid "980f54b2-460e-59b5-8818-a3f1c522f319" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4cbbd6-8c6a-5f19-82b0-fb814880d5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448230} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fe2f8d-3d94-55f1-943e-f623b35fa6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce4cbbd6-8c6a-5f19-82b0-fb814880d5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448230} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a18d9fc-a32b-5b63-b411-bd741ac1d6fe" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a9fbb00-26f5-5603-96a1-9b8b6773d49d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125789" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,1⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction” is true. Parameterizing the circle, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{1}{3},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨1-t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨(1-t)^{2}+{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc24743-a02f-5bf0-9839-ac80348908a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448232} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e9b1d4-bf42-5627-ad17-aca33e0e39b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fc24743-a02f-5bf0-9839-ac80348908a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448232} :answer-field/id #uuid "584fe0e6-b857-54a2-9215-9de39aae4c49" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0dcb42-65e9-55a9-904d-f11371368f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448234} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd98ec7-8666-52b5-b988-4cbc4f87a080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd98ec7-8666-52b5-b988-4cbc4f87a080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448235} :answer-field/id #uuid "2031bd8d-6dd0-50f1-a070-e8d5e67ed206" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd954a98-2cf9-5746-8938-2c98f9e007e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3d7e0e-e48d-5344-a89f-52e9913c3871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd954a98-2cf9-5746-8938-2c98f9e007e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448236} :answer-field/id #uuid "27965924-fed2-57f3-875b-59e349ddc1fc" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449158} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84969817-3dcd-5fdb-8d9f-412b153421cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448238} {:answer/id #uuid "16347b9d-3533-5b71-8f9e-64d4326612b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84969817-3dcd-5fdb-8d9f-412b153421cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448238} :answer-field/id #uuid "065de688-3909-5264-8ef5-ac4a05f8d35e" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fb181d0-594b-53e9-a69e-a9a120629b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125791" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{3{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc80e04-300d-575e-befb-8d927f5a1393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463542} {:answer/id #uuid "11a2cad8-574b-5542-b2bb-a30e735d1d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11a2cad8-574b-5542-b2bb-a30e735d1d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463543} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6de86d3-8240-540a-9b53-90a7214df88d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc0b456-6310-5ad5-b10c-eb2af4c2fc70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463544} {:answer/id #uuid "84cbd523-8193-5a7a-b8cd-96a8abf8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dc0b456-6310-5ad5-b10c-eb2af4c2fc70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463544} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1caf6b-e6da-5368-a8d4-722507ce7684" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be16c156-3162-5023-ad80-da11f2f6bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463546} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8d6bcb-9ee4-5850-8817-08b222a8bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be16c156-3162-5023-ad80-da11f2f6bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463546} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cc552f-f6da-53d0-8115-14a52ecbc6c5" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af2b663-f664-5687-8b3d-4cb7ed3e89ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463548} {:answer/id #uuid "12a3b8bc-c982-5ad8-998b-0a6fb0036f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af2b663-f664-5687-8b3d-4cb7ed3e89ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463548} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b4e1b4-58bf-5e0a-bc9c-2b1223fdcc3c" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "528091bc-3b41-5908-b5b6-b20261706098" :question/math-academy-id "q-125792" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨1,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(e,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}⟨1,\\,t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}1+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t(e-1)+1,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,t(e-1)+1⟩\\cdot ⟨e-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}e-1+t(e-1)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(e-1)+e\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}(e-1)}{2}+et]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{2}+e \\\\  & =\\frac{3e-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6341e8be-459b-5b87-936b-1dfe23420804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448240} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3748af-eec3-5d38-b136-c6716c918337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6341e8be-459b-5b87-936b-1dfe23420804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448240} :answer-field/id #uuid "9201a5f2-4cf8-5f69-a141-20ed8f68831c" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d894e19b-da48-587a-8286-f5de79b1260f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448242} {:answer/id #uuid "c44d9fc1-5023-5b92-bc78-6410e5c51c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44d9fc1-5023-5b92-bc78-6410e5c51c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448243} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa0a59f-3168-5a4c-88fd-9425997ad0d1" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449161} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e55a1e-ab9d-569a-96fc-f107b33b92d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448244} {:answer/id #uuid "0dddf6eb-6699-5d37-8672-fadcf8148fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61e55a1e-ab9d-569a-96fc-f107b33b92d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448244} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cb0ecc-4dd2-5c8b-8667-c8f0701fba2d" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a4a1778-edf5-5213-be03-411dc4e3fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448246} {:answer/id #uuid "1660cf69-a660-5241-9712-d3ea386c916f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a4a1778-edf5-5213-be03-411dc4e3fabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448246} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc39a85a-6de3-550f-a6b3-e9e0188c695d" :answer-field/key "$F$ is conservative." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da972c83-042d-5f07-b576-d951f35ad923" :question/math-academy-id "q-125793" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(4,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨4t,\\,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨4{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16{t}^{2}-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{[\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =8-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13264dd3-32ca-5425-ae4a-10ae592d25f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260044} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4d041-01f8-5cba-bbc5-711d818eb654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692} {:answer/id #uuid "7344edf5-4fa2-53cc-aefd-2082b97c5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} {:answer/id #uuid "deb142df-e54d-5f60-a501-f62bc2ccccc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} :answer-field/id #uuid "6208b069-a1cc-5b39-96a3-5295dfbd12c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84980841-460b-507f-8790-439efc8ff63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e93c4c-784f-5084-ab8a-83b41a9c3b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259887} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd94e5-4741-54cd-91f5-397f8394cacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260121} {:answer/id #uuid "26253928-a96d-5ca2-ada8-659d943f2a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260209} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} {:answer/id #uuid "d06bbafa-a9ef-5c6e-ae57-d690593cedb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b35c4ee-3674-511e-b02e-606a2c68404b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d73aed01-512e-5f02-83f5-67f605d164e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126526" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e6/e69bc348e36b05955d1eaa9eda4516afe137056ead176a46a4fc11bb887ed166.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad86e3ef-c12c-5d63-830a-dabac47cd90c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261396} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96fb47-5ab0-591e-bc64-e12732e133be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261400} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd7a97a-dfe1-5328-9d66-c8db33781a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261528} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbe8614-b318-5962-bad4-61b3cb58bbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261819} {:answer/id #uuid "fd16fd7d-6e48-5c28-b4cd-957ff093701a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad96fb47-5ab0-591e-bc64-e12732e133be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261400} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0748a2-8172-58a7-b068-6319c44ba0db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3ea6616-7ac7-5c42-90f8-53e0ef7d6fff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126529" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+{y}^{2})\\,i+y{e}^{x}\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1,1)} & =(1){e}^{1}-2(1) \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3041df57-595f-539a-b239-3c78801fee42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260309} {:answer/id #uuid "4b114062-2293-5f00-a193-d448102a6e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260520} {:answer/id #uuid "55005e13-3fcc-5508-8bcc-edecfb139184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260598} {:answer/id #uuid "5caebef8-ad28-5626-af16-e744946586b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260668} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2b0c46-2ff3-54df-b539-763bc499f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55005e13-3fcc-5508-8bcc-edecfb139184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260598} :answer-field/id #uuid "71103381-771b-5d7a-9427-0631a58fb2d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a832124-e7c1-5d36-a8d7-a22e220546d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126535" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+(2x+{y}^{3})\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
